COPYRIGHT NOTICE — NO LICENCE IS GRANTED
========================================

The first-party code and documentation in this repository originate with
University College London, as the SGNL Orbit Prediction Software (2017b),
itself a 2014 rewrite of the pre-2010 UCL Orbit Dynamics Library.

**No copyright or licence statement existed anywhere in the original tree** —
not in `README.md`, not in any file header, and there was no `LICENSE` file.
Nothing in this repository establishes who holds the rights in that work or who
is entitled to license it.

This notice does not assert who the copyright holder is, because that has not
been established. It records that the work is somebody's, most likely UCL's,
and that **no licence is granted here by anyone.**

You may not copy, modify, distribute, or use this code except as the copyright
holder permits or the law allows. If you want to do any of those things, the
question of who can give you permission has to be settled first, and this
repository is not where that happens.

Withdrawn: the PolyForm Noncommercial offer
-------------------------------------------

Between 15 and 16 September 2026 this file offered the PolyForm Noncommercial
License 1.0.0. That offer is withdrawn, and this is stated rather than quietly
deleted so that anyone who saw the earlier state knows it changed and why: an
offer that may not be the offeror's to make is worse than no offer, and
withdrawing it costs less than leaving it standing.

What that costs, stated plainly
-------------------------------

The withdrawn licence permitted research, teaching and personal use. This one
permits nothing, so nobody can now rely on it to run this software and
reproduce the results in `REVIVAL.md` — including the LightSail-2 laser-ranging
fit, which is the part of this work worth reproducing. That is a real cost of
the conservative position and not an oversight.

What it does not change
-----------------------

This repository is public on GitHub, and GitHub's own terms of service let
anyone who can see a public repository view it and fork it within GitHub,
whatever this file says. "No licence granted" is therefore narrower in practice
than it sounds: it governs use, modification and distribution outside those
terms, and it is not a claim that nobody may look.

It also does not reach the third-party code vendored under `external/` or the
third-party data under `res/`. Each of those keeps the terms of whoever
published it, unaffected by anything here. `NOTICE` lists them individually;
`PROVENANCE.md` traces every file to where it came from.
